<Project>

  <!--
    C15's fence. The relay's container smoke test has to dial wss:// at a throwaway self-signed
    certificate, so it disables certificate validation on its client socket. That line is the risk,
    not the certificate: a client that accepts any certificate has no transport authentication and
    still LOOKS protected, which is worse than plain ws:// and is what D-11 exists to prevent.

    Before C15 this repository contained no such line anywhere. That is the only moment fencing one
    off is free, so it is fenced now rather than after the second copy exists. It is the most
    copy-pasted line in .NET networking, and the next person debugging a self-hosted relay would
    otherwise find it in our own test project and carry it into the plugin — where it would silently
    undo the transport guarantee RelayEndpoint's TLS requirement is there to make.

    A comment saying "test only" is not a control; it is the thing that gets copied along with the
    line. This is a control, and the next paragraph is the rest of the sentence.

    WHAT IT ACTUALLY CATCHES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It matches the NAMES below, textually, line by
    line. It does not understand C#, so a POSITIONAL callback — an SslStream constructed with a
    validation delegate passed as an argument rather than assigned to a named property — is NOT
    caught, and neither is a delegate reached through a variable or a helper. Closing that needs a
    Roslyn analyser with the semantic model; it is not a longer token list, and adding tokens will
    keep looking like progress while the hole stays exactly where it is.

    Say so here, because a guard people trust further than it reaches is the failure mode — worse
    than the gap itself. What this buys is the copy-paste route, which is the one that actually
    happens.

    More edges, recorded rather than fixed:

      - The allowlist's granularity is the PROJECT. All of DungeonMasterXIV.Relay.Tests is unfenced,
        not just the one method that needs it, so a second test there could reach for the same line —
        including the process-global ServicePointManager form, which would affect every test in the
        run. A file-level allowlist would match the actual intent.

      - The comment stripper is a REGEX, not a C# lexer. It protects raw, verbatim, ordinary and
        character literals so that a comment marker inside one cannot open a fake comment, which is
        the failure that matters: an over-eager strip removes real code and the fence goes SILENT
        over it. Measured against nine specimens (see TlsBypassFenceTests) — a naive strip of just
        '/\*[\s\S]*?\*/' blinds the fence on four of them, this one on none.

        What it does not model: interpolated strings whose holes contain quotes, and nested raw
        string literals. Neither appears in this solution. If one arrives and carries an unbalanced
        comment marker, the fence could go quiet over the rest of that file — so this is the thing
        to re-check before writing such a literal, and the reason the specimen list is a test rather
        than a comment.

      - %(Compile) is silent on anything that is not compiled C# from disk, notably source-generated
        files. There are no generators in this solution today.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      An ALLOWLIST of the projects permitted to hold a bypass, for C18's reason: naming what is
      forbidden goes stale the first time somebody adds a project, and the new project passes
      cleanly. Naming what is permitted means a project added tomorrow is scanned by default.
    -->
    <TlsValidationBypassPermittedIn>DungeonMasterXIV.Relay.Tests</TlsValidationBypassPermittedIn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The comment stripper. Alternation order is the whole design: the four literal forms are tried
    FIRST and captured, so '$1$2$3$4' puts them back; the two comment forms are tried last and
    capture nothing, so they substitute to empty and vanish. That is how a comment gets removed and
    a string containing "wss://" or "/*" survives, without needing a MatchEvaluator MSBuild cannot
    give us.

    Kept as a property rather than inlined so it can be read. A guard nobody can read is a guard
    nobody maintains.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TlsFenceCommentPattern>("""[\s\S]*?""")|(@"(?:[^"]|"")*")|("(?:\\.|[^"\\])*")|('(?:\\.|[^'\\])*')|/\*[\s\S]*?\*/|//[^\r\n]*</TlsFenceCommentPattern>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="NoTlsValidationBypass"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Outputs="%(Compile.Identity)"
          Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' != '$(TlsValidationBypassPermittedIn)'">

    <!--
      ONE BATCH PER FILE, and that is what BUG-18 turned on. The scan used to flatten every line of
      every file into one list, so a line had no way to ask whether it sat inside a block comment —
      and an interior line of plain indented prose was scanned as though it were code, failing the
      build for a comment that told people NOT to do the thing.

      Batching per Compile item means the file's own text is in hand, so the question is answered
      once for the file instead of guessed at per line.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ScannedSourceLine Remove="@(_ScannedSourceLine)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      THE DECISION. Comments removed, literals kept. A token that survives this is in code or in a
      string, and both of those fire: a bypass name in a string literal is either reflection reaching
      for it or documentation that should have been a comment, and the conservative reading is the
      safe one for a guard whose failure mode is silence.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_CodeOutsideComments>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace($([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('%(Compile.FullPath)')), '$(TlsFenceCommentPattern)', '$1$2$3$4'))</_CodeOutsideComments>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      Reads the source rather than the reference graph, unlike ForbidDalamud and NoDurableStorage:
      a bypass arrives as a line somebody typed, not as a dependency somebody added.

      EVERY NAME HERE IS ONE YOU ASSIGN A CALLBACK TO IN ORDER TO STOP VALIDATING. None of them is a
      substring of a hardening spelling, and that is a property to re-check before adding one:
      CheckCertificateRevocation was in this list, and it is a contiguous substring of
      CheckCertificateRevocationList — the property you set TRUE to make revocation checking
      STRICTER. So the guard failed the build on code that hardened TLS, with a message asserting
      the author had disabled it. An instrument that produces false FAILs is worse than one that
      cannot fail: the first person to hit it concludes the guard is noise, and a noisy guard gets
      relaxed. The weakening spelling is deliberately NOT matched as a bare name; catching it would
      mean matching an assignment of false, and a token list cannot see an assignment.
    -->
    <ReadLinesFromFile File="%(Compile.FullPath)" Condition="$(_CodeOutsideComments.Contains('RemoteCertificateValidationCallback'))
                                                          Or $(_CodeOutsideComments.Contains('ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback'))
                                                          Or $(_CodeOutsideComments.Contains('ServerCertificateValidationCallback'))
                                                          Or $(_CodeOutsideComments.Contains('userCertificateValidationCallback'))
                                                          Or $(_CodeOutsideComments.Contains('DangerousAccept'))">
      <Output TaskParameter="Lines" ItemName="_ScannedSourceLine" />
    </ReadLinesFromFile>

    <!--
      The line is quoted, not decided on. The decision was made above, over the whole file; this
      only picks which line to show the reader, and the comment prefixes are here so it does not
      pick a comment line in a file that also holds a real bypass. Do not mistake this for a second
      gate — widening it cannot unfence anything, and narrowing it cannot fence anything.
    -->
    <Error Condition="!$([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').TrimStart().StartsWith('//'))
                  And !$([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').TrimStart().StartsWith('/*'))
                  And !$([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').TrimStart().StartsWith('* '))
                  And ($([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').Contains('RemoteCertificateValidationCallback'))
                    Or $([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').Contains('ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback'))
                    Or $([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').Contains('ServerCertificateValidationCallback'))
                    Or $([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').Contains('userCertificateValidationCallback'))
                    Or $([System.String]::Copy('%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)').Contains('DangerousAccept')))"
           Text="$(MSBuildProjectName) disables TLS certificate validation in %(Compile.Identity): &quot;%(_ScannedSourceLine.Identity)&quot;. A client that accepts any certificate has no transport authentication and still looks protected (D-11). Only $(TlsValidationBypassPermittedIn) may do this, and only to reach the throwaway certificate C15's container smoke test mints." />

  </Target>

</Project>
